2. PROBLEM FRAMING & ONTOLOGY DESIGN
================================================================================
Problem Statement:
  Supply chain data is fragmented across ERP, logistics, and planning silos.
  Three different personas ask "What is our on-time delivery rate?" and get
  conflicting answers:
    - Procurement: 91% (measured against supplier promised delivery date)
    - Logistics:   88% (measured against carrier transit SLA)
    - Planning:    82% (measured against customer requested date)
  A raw LLM chatbot exacerbates this by hallucinating single-table queries
  with integer division errors (returning 0.0%).

Solution Design:
  Build a unified Supply Chain Ontology expressed as a Snowflake Semantic View
  and Cortex Analyst Semantic Model YAML over 7 tables:
    DIM_SUPPLIER -> DIM_PART -> DIM_PLANT -> FCT_PURCHASE_ORDERS -> FCT_SHIPMENTS -> FCT_CUSTOMER_ORDERS -> DIM_CUSTOMER

Canonical Metrics Defined:
  1. Inbound Supplier OTD (Procurement Lens): receipt_date <= promised_date
  2. Carrier Transit OTD (Logistics Lens): actual_delivery_date <= estimated_delivery_date
  3. Customer OTIF (Planning Lens): shipped_date <= request_date AND shipped_qty >= ordered_qty
  4. Enterprise Canonical OTD (Executive Lens): Weighted multi-channel average
  5. Fill Rate: order fulfillment percentage
  6. Landed Cost: unit_cost + freight/unit + customs/unit
  7. Days of Inventory (DOI proxy)

